{"causes": ["The institutionalization of the Roman Catholic Church provided a structured path for ambitious and educated individuals like Guillaume de Bray to ascend to positions of power.", "The growth of papal power and the emergence of the College of Cardinals as a central governing body created opportunities for prominent ecclesiastics to exert significant influence across Christendom.", "The flourishing of Gothic art and architecture across Western Europe fostered an environment where wealthy and influential figures could become major patrons of monumental artistic works."], "effects": ["Guillaume de Bray's patronage led to the creation of one of the most significant sculptural monuments of the late 13th century by Arnolfo di Cambio.", "His participation in papal conclaves, including the election of Martin IV, influenced the leadership and direction of the Roman Catholic Church during a critical period.", "De Bray's diplomatic activities and counsel helped to maintain papal authority and stability amidst complex political struggles in medieval Italy and France."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "arnolfo-di-cambio", "sourceName": "Arnolfo di Cambio", "verb": "CREATES", "targetSlug": "guillaume-de-bray", "targetName": "Guillaume de Bray", "context": "Arnolfo di Cambio sculpted the monumental tomb for Guillaume de Bray in San Domenico Maggiore, Naples, after 1282."}, {"sourceSlug": "guillaume-de-bray", "sourceName": "Guillaume de Bray", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "papal-conclave-1281", "targetName": "Papal Conclave of 1281", "context": "Cardinal Guillaume de Bray was one of the cardinals who participated in the conclave of 1281 in Viterbo, which elected Pope Martin IV."}, {"sourceSlug": "guillaume-de-bray", "sourceName": "Guillaume de Bray", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "orvieto-italy", "targetName": "Orvieto, Italy", "context": "Guillaume de Bray died in Orvieto on April 29, 1282, a city frequently serving as a papal residence during his lifetime."}, {"sourceSlug": "pope-martin-iv", "sourceName": "Pope Martin IV", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "guillaume-de-bray", "targetName": "Guillaume de Bray", "context": "Pope Martin IV's election and subsequent pontificate significantly influenced Guillaume de Bray's later career, as de Bray was a close associate and supporter."}, {"sourceSlug": "guillaume-de-bray", "sourceName": "Guillaume de Bray", "verb": "TRANSMITS", "targetSlug": "gothic-sculpture", "targetName": "Gothic Sculpture", "context": "Through his commissioned tomb, Guillaume de Bray helped transmit and exemplify the evolving styles of Italian Gothic sculpture in the late 13th century."}], "places": [{"name": "Naples, Italy", "role": "Burial site; location of monumental tomb"}, {"name": "Orvieto, Italy", "role": "Place of death"}, {"name": "Viterbo, Italy", "role": "Site of papal conclaves"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Guillaume de Bray", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "guillaume-de-bray"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q3120293", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guillaume_de_Bray"], "tags": ["France", "Religious Figures", "Europe", "comparative-religion", "doctrine-development", "cultural-diffusion"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Guillaume de Bray holds significant regional and thematic importance due to his influential role as a cardinal in the 13th-century papal curia and, more enduringly, as the subject of one of Arnolfo di Cambio's most iconic Gothic tombs, which shaped artistic trends.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-31T19:38:29Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#30659518457", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Guillaume de Bray was a French ecclesiastic and Roman Catholic Cardinal, poet, and mathematician.", "newValue": "Guillaume de Bray, a prominent French ecclesiastic, rose through the ranks of the Roman Catholic Church to become Cardinal-Priest of San Marco, serving under several popes during the tumultuous latter half of the 13th century. Born in France at an unknown date, de Bray’s career epitomized the intersection of high medieval church politics, diplomacy, and intellectual patronage. His influence extended across Western Europe, particularly within the papal curia, where he was a trusted advisor and active participant in critical Church affairs until his death in Orvieto on April 29, 1282.

De Bray's significant contributions included his participation in multiple papal conclaves, notably the 1281 election of Pope Martin IV, whom he had previously served as a close associate. Beyond his ecclesiastical duties, he was a notable patron of the arts and letters, fostering a vibrant intellectual environment within his sphere. His most enduring legacy in this realm is his magnificent tomb in San Domenico Maggiore, Naples, commissioned by his brother Pierre de Bray and executed by the renowned sculptor Arnolfo di Cambio. This monumental work, featuring a Madonna enthroned, stands as a pivotal example of late 13th-century Italian Gothic sculpture, blending classical influences with emerging naturalism.

